while inflation in the united states has  hit 9.1 percent there's plenty of other  places around the world that have way  higher inflation and people are  suffering let's take argentina as a  quintessential example it is estimated  that by the end of this year argentinian  citizens may be experiencing inflation  as high as 90 percent year over year  that 90 inflation means that their money  is getting cut in half in its purchasing  power on an annual basis this is  completely unsustainable for the south  american country which is not only a  massive part of the continent but also  houses tens of millions of citizens  as we begin to think about the people on  the ground in a 90 inflation environment  prices change day to day it's nearly  impossible to plan your life and all you  think about is how can you get out of  the currency so that you can buy some  goods buy store of value or somehow some  way protect the economic value that you  go to work for and that you worked so  hard for here's some quotes from the  bloomberg article argentines face the  prospect of 90 inflation by year end  after the economy ministers exit  triggered overnight price rises and the  central bank comes under pressure to let  the peso depreciate more rapidly that'd  be the fastest pace since hyperinflation  three decades ago and the highest rate  in the world outside of venezuela and  sudan according to forecast from the imf  the dramatic exit of former economy  minister martin guzman this month led to  price markups by many businesses some  argentines raced to the shops the  morning after guzman quit to try to  stock up ahead of peso devaluation and  price hikes now that 90 inflation number  is important it'll grab all the  headlines but there's another aspect of  the argentine economy that is important  to understand they have implemented  price controls on more than 1 000  different goods within their economy any  time that you see price controls being  implemented it means that politicians  central bankers and people in positions  of power and influence are starting to  intervene even more into the market they  claim that they're doing it for a good  reason that they want to stop price  gouging or that they want to help the  average citizen but what we know is that  the free market can solve problems much  faster than an intervention driven  monetary policy or solution and so  regardless of what politicians say price  controls are a bad bad decision and as  we watch in argentina there are price  controls on a lot of goods but inflation  remains out of control  all you're doing is you're putting more  stress on the system and you're not  actually solving the problem i have no  clue what the right solution is in  argentina it looks like inflation is on  a runaway path the people on the ground  are suffering  and not only do they understand  inflation really well because out of  necessity they've had to get a crash  course  when they walk into the grocery store or  they try and protect that purchasing  power but also there's pain that doesn't  show up in the economic data people lose  their life savings people lose their  businesses and literally people go  hungry because they can't afford the  skyrocketing cost of food whatever ends  up being the solution i hope it comes  fast because living in a high inflation  environment of 90 percent  is completely unsustainable for your  average family
 

